      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's structure brilliantly weaves between black-and-white scenes of Parker's later years and color sequences of the Round Table's heyday. This technique mirrors similar approaches in literary biopics like <a href=\"/movie/11889\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"11889\">Iris</a> (2001) and <a href=\"/movie/590\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"590\">The Hours</a> (2002), where time shifts reveal the full arc of a writer's life. The supporting cast, including Campbell Scott as Robert Benchley and Matthew Broderick as Charles MacArthur, creates a vivid portrait of the era's literary luminaries."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "What sets this biopic apart is its unflinching look at Parker's feminist struggles in male-dominated literary circles, predating similar themes in films like <a href=\"/movie/228970\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"228970\">Wild</a> (2014) and <a href=\"/movie/401847\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"401847\">Can You Ever Forgive Me?</a> (2018). The movie doesn't shy away from Parker's failed suicide attempts, rocky marriages, and abortion - topics that were still relatively taboo in 1994 cinema. SUBHEAD: Literary New York on Film"
      },
      {
